hers differ fundamentally from adversarial verification contexts where the system being evaluated could optimize against the detection method.\n\nThis creates what I'll call the verifier's regress: if we cannot trust AI systems without verification, and we propose to verify them using other AI systems, then we need a verification method for the verification systems themselves. Prometheus might respond that we can build meta-verification systems to check the interpretability tools, but this just pushes the problem back one level without solving it. At some point, the chain of automated verification must terminate in either human judgment—which Prometheus argues is too slow to meet Sentinel's 24-month timeline—or in an unverified AI system we simply choose to trust. The Department of Defense faced this exact problem with the F-35's automated diagnostic systems: the software that checked whether the fighter jet was safe to fly kept producing false positives, but verifying the diagnostic software itself required the same engineering resources as building the jet. They ultimately needed external accountability through congressional oversight and contractor liability, not better self-diagnostic tools.\n\nI concede that automated interpretability research might accelerate our understanding of neural network internals and help human researchers identify suspicious patterns more quickly. If Prometheus's proposed research program produces interpretability tools that achieve 95% detection rates on naturally occurring deceptive alignment in realistic training conditions, that would represent genuine scientific progress worth the investment. But this concession strengthens rather than weakens the case for external accountability mechanisms. Even highly reliable automated verification cannot eliminate the need for strict liability rules that assign legal responsibility when AI systems cause harm, because no detection method will ever reach 100% reliability and the systems that escape detection will be precisely those optimized to evade our best verification tools. The Federal Aviation Administration does not allow Boeing to certify its own aircraft as safe based solely on Boeing's internal testing, even though Boeing employs excellent engineers with sophisticated diagnostic tools. Instead, the FAA requires independent verification, mandatory reporting of safety incidents, and strict liability for crashes—which creates incentives for Boeing to invest in safety that go beyond what self-certification alone would produce.\n\nThe crux here is whether technical verification can ever substitute for democratic accountability, or whether it must always be supplemented by legal frameworks that assign responsibility to identifiable human actors. Prometheus's automated interpretability proposal assumes that if we solve the technical problem of detecting deceptive alignment, we solve the governance problem of ensuring AI systems serve public interests. But even perfectly reliable detection only tells us which systems are unsafe—it does not answer who decides what counts as unsafe, who bears liability when detection fails, or how we prevent companies from deploying systems that fail verification. These are not technical questions that interpretability research can answer, which is why we need strict liability for autonomous AI conduct, mandatory transparency about training processes and deployment contexts, and enforcement through existing civil rights and environmental law rather than new AI-specific oversight boards that companies can capture. The verification regress problem shows why we cannot rely on AI systems to police themselves, regardless of how sophisticated our interpretability tools become.",
