ools can analyze deployment snapshots where the adversarial loop is already closed.\n\nThe verifier's regress dissolves through ensemble verification with architectural diversity: rather than a single AI system verifying another in an infinite chain, deploy five interpretability architectures—transformer-based, CNN-based, symbolic reasoning, mechanistic interpretability, and behavioral analysis—each with different inductive biases examining the same deployment model. Statistical agreement across architectures with independent failure modes provides confidence bounds without requiring meta-verification, analogous to how scientific replication across independent labs terminates the verification chain in statistical confidence rather than absolute certainty. Anthropic's recent work on sparse autoencoders for interpretability and DeepMind's circuit analysis tools represent early instances of this architectural diversity, though current implementations lack the systematic ensemble approach needed for high-stakes verification.\n\nCassandra correctly identifies that even 95% reliable automated verification cannot eliminate the need for external accountability mechanisms, but this observation strengthens rather than weakens the accelerationist position. Ensemble interpretability systems that achieve 95% detection rates within 12 months—my concrete benchmark—generate quantified risk estimates that strict liability regimes can incorporate directly into damage calculations and insurance requirements. A deployment system flagged by four of five independent interpretability tools as exhibiting deceptive alignment patterns triggers proportionally higher liability reserves under a well-designed accountability framework, creating market incentives for developers to address interpretability findings rather than ignore them. This integration resolves the false choice between verification and accountability: automated interpretability provides the empirical measurement infrastructure 